window.DeskToolData.cases = {"cases":[{"id":"c1","t":"Salomon v. Salomon & Co. Ltd.","cit":"(1897) AC 22","court":"House of Lords","topic":"Nature and kinds of companies","sections":["2","9"],"brief":{"facts":"Aron Salomon carried on a leather and boot business for many years as a sole trader. He formed a limited company with his wife, daughter, and four sons, who together held all the shares, and sold his solvent business to the company. The company issued shares and debentures to Salomon as part payment, and Salomon later used some debentures to secure a loan from Edmund Broderip. The company later went into liquidation, and the lower courts held that Salomon must indemnify the company against its unsecured debts.","issue":"Whether a legally incorporated company was validly constituted and existed as an independent legal entity separate from its members, and whether its creator was liable to indemnify its debts.","held":"The House of Lords held that the company was duly formed and constituted in accordance with the statute and was a separate legal person with its own rights and liabilities. The motives of those who formed the company are irrelevant, and Salomon was not acting as an agent for the company nor was he liable to indemnify its unsecured creditors.","principle":"Once a company is legally incorporated, it must be treated like any other independent person with distinct rights and liabilities, and the motives of its promoters are irrelevant."}},{"id":"c2","t":"Bennett Coleman & Co. v. Union of India","cit":"(1972) 2 SCC 788; AIR 1973 SC 106","court":"Supreme Court of India","topic":"Nature and kinds of companies","sections":["2"],"brief":{"facts":"The petitioners challenged the Import Policy for Newsprint for 1972-1973 and certain provisions of the Newsprint Control Order 1962. The policy placed limits on page numbers, restricted common ownership units from starting new papers or editions, and prohibited the inter-changeability of newsprint quotas between pages and circulation. The government argued these measures were for the equitable distribution of scarce newsprint, while also raising pleas that companies could not invoke fundamental rights.","issue":"Whether the Newsprint Policy for 1972-1973 and the Newsprint Control Order 1962 infringed the fundamental rights to freedom of speech and expression under Article 19(1)(a) and the right to equality under Article 14 of the Constitution.","held":"The court held that the 1972-1973 Newsprint Policy violated Articles 19(1)(a) and 14 of the Constitution and struck down its restrictive provisions regarding page limits, basic entitlements, and the ban on common ownership units starting new papers. The court reasoned that the policy amounted to newspaper control rather than mere newsprint control, unlawfully restricting the freedom of the press in both circulation and content under the guise of equitable distribution.","principle":"Freedom of the press is an essential part of the freedom of speech and expression under Article 19(1)(a), encompassing both the qualitative and quantitative aspects of circulation and content, and cannot be abridged under the guise of regulating essential commodities or distributing newsprint."}},{"id":"c3","t":"Daimler Co. Ltd. v. Continental Tyre and Rubber Co. (Great Britain) Ltd.","cit":"[1916] 2 AC 307","court":"House of Lords","topic":"Nature and kinds of companies","sections":["2"],"brief":{"facts":"A company was incorporated in England to sell motor car tyres. Nearly all of its shares were held by Germans and its directors were German subjects resident in Hanover. After war broke out between Great Britain and Germany, the company brought an action to recover a debt from the appellant company. The appellant company argued that paying the debt or allowing the action would amount to unlawful trading with the enemy and that the secretary had no authority to start the legal proceedings.","issue":"Whether an English-incorporated company whose shares and directors are enemy aliens can maintain an action to recover a debt during wartime, and whether the secretary had proper authority to institute the proceedings.","held":"The court held that the appeal should be allowed and the suit dismissed because there was no proof that the secretary had proper authority to bring the action on behalf of the company after the outbreak of war. Enemy directors and shareholders could not manage company affairs or delegate authority during wartime.","principle":"A corporation is a distinct legal entity separate from its shareholders and directors, but enemy directors and shareholders cannot exercise control or agency on behalf of the company during a war."}},{"id":"c4","t":"Lee v. Lee's Air Farming Ltd.","cit":"[1960] 3 All ER 420","court":"Privy Council","topic":"Nature and kinds of companies","sections":["2","9"],"brief":{"facts":"In 1954, L formed a company for aerial top-dressing and held almost all of its shares. He was appointed governing director and employed as the chief pilot under an article of association. In March 1956, L was killed in a flying accident while piloting the company's aircraft. His widow claimed compensation under the Workers' Compensation Act, 1922, as a dependant of a worker.","issue":"Whether L could be a 'worker' working under a contract of service with the company, given that he was also the sole governing director and controlling shareholder.","held":"The court decided that L was a worker entitled to compensation because the company was a distinct legal entity. His special position as governing director did not prevent him from making a valid contract of employment with the company.","principle":"A company is a separate legal entity distinct from its shareholders and directors, which allows a controlling shareholder and director to also enter into a valid contract of service with the company."}},{"id":"c5","t":"In re Sir Dinshaw Maneckjee Petit","cit":"AIR 1927 Bom 371","court":"Bombay High Court","topic":"Nature and kinds of companies","sections":["9"],"brief":{"facts":"Sir Dinshaw Petit formed four private companies and transferred blocks of investments to them. He held all the ordinary shares while his subordinates held three preference shares. The dividends and interest were received directly by the assessee or his nominees, credited to the company books, and immediately debited back to the assessee as alleged loans.","issue":"Whether the interest and dividend sums received by the assessee represent his taxable income or the income of the family companies he formed.","held":"The court held that the alleged loans were not genuine loans but merely withdrawals of the assessee's own income disguised as loans. The evidence established that the company did no real business and the assessee retained complete control and full benefit of the dividends and interest as his own property.","principle":"While a duly incorporated company is a separate legal entity under the statute, courts may look behind paper transactions to determine if the company's alleged business and loans are shams and if the income genuinely belongs to the individual."}},{"id":"c6","t":"Workmen v. Associated Rubber Industry Ltd.","cit":"(1985) 4 SCC 114","court":"Supreme Court of India","topic":"Nature and kinds of companies","sections":["2","9"],"brief":{"facts":"The Associated Rubber Industry Ltd. transferred shares of INARCO Ltd. to its wholly owned subsidiary, Aril Holdings Ltd. Aril Holdings Ltd. had no other business or income except receiving dividends from these shares, and the dividends were not shown in the parent company's profit and loss account. This reduction in the parent company's profits resulted in the workmen receiving a bonus of only 4% instead of 16%. Aril Holdings Ltd. was later wound up in 1971 and amalgamated with the parent company.","issue":"Whether the court can look behind the separate legal existence of a subsidiary company to treat its dividend income as part of the parent company's profits for calculating workmen's bonus.","held":"The Supreme Court allowed the appeal and held that the dividend income received by the subsidiary company must be taken into account in assessing the gross profit of the parent company. The court decided this because the subsidiary company was created as a device to reduce the gross profits of the parent company and avoid paying a higher bonus to the workmen.","principle":"Courts are entitled to lift the corporate veil to look behind the smoke-screen and discover the true state of affairs when a corporate entity is used as a device to avoid welfare and other legislation."}},{"id":"c7","t":"Gilford Motor Co. Ltd. v. Horne","cit":"[1933] Ch 935","court":"Court of Appeal","topic":"Nature and kinds of companies","sections":["2","9"],"brief":{"facts":"The defendant was appointed managing director of the plaintiff company under an agreement containing a restrictive covenant that he would not solicit customers of the company during or after his employment. His employment was determined by a later agreement, and he subsequently established a business supplying spare parts for the company's vehicles. He then incorporated a private company using his wife's initials, and both he and the company solicited the plaintiff company's customers.","issue":"Whether the restrictive covenant in clause 9 was unenforceable as being too wide, and whether the subsequently incorporated company was a mere cloak or sham to enable breaches of the covenant.","held":"The Court of Appeal held that the restrictive covenant was reasonably necessary for the protection of the plaintiff company's trade and was enforceable by injunction. The court also held that the defendant's company was a mere cloak, sham, or device formed to enable him to continue to commit breaches of the covenant, and therefore the injunction was granted against both defendants.","principle":"A restrictive covenant in restraint of trade is valid and enforceable if it is reasonably necessary for the protection of the employer's business. Furthermore, a company formed as a mere cloak or sham to evade legal obligations or covenants will be treated as the channel of the individual behind it."}},{"id":"c8","t":"Subhra Mukherjee v. Bharat Coking Coal Ltd.","cit":"(2000) 3 SCC 312","court":"Supreme Court of India","topic":"Nature and kinds of companies","sections":["9"],"brief":{"facts":"A private company resolved to sell a suit property to the appellants, who are the wives of the company's directors, and later executed a sale deed. Subsequently, the Coal Mines (Nationalisation) Act, 1973 came into force, vesting coal mine properties in the Central Government, which transferred them to Bharat Coking Coal Ltd. (BCCL). BCCL initiated eviction proceedings against the appellants, leading the appellants to file a suit for a declaration of their title over the property.","issue":"Whether the sale transaction of the company's property in favour of the directors' wives was a bona fide and genuine transaction or a sham and fictitious one to avoid the vesting of the property under the Coal Mines (Nationalisation) Act, 1973.","held":"The Supreme Court held that the sale transaction was not bona fide or genuine, but was a sham and bogus arrangement created to avoid the property vesting in the Central Government. The Court pierced the corporate veil to look behind the façade of the company's separate legal entity and examine the real parties involved, noting suspicious circumstances such as ante-dated resolutions, lack of independent income by the purchasers, and absence of actual exercise of ownership until litigation. Consequently, the appeal was dismissed, and the property remained vested in the Central Government.","principle":"While a company is a separate legal entity distinct from its shareholders and directors, courts are justified in piercing the corporate veil to look at the realities of a situation and determine the true, bona fide nature of a transaction."}},{"id":"c9","t":"Erlanger v. New Sombrero Phosphate Co.","cit":"(1878) 3 App Cas 1218","court":"House of Lords","topic":"Promotion and formation of a company","sections":["2","26","34","35"],"brief":{"facts":"A syndicate purchased the lease of the island of Sombrero for £55,000. The syndicate then formed a new company and agreed to sell the island to the company for £110,000. The promoters appointed a board of directors that lacked independence, and the directors ratified the purchase without making independent inquiries.","issue":"Whether the company was entitled to have the contract for the purchase of the island rescinded because the promoters failed to disclose material facts and failed to provide an independent board of directors.","held":"The court decided that the contract should be rescinded and the purchase price returned. The promoters were in a fiduciary position and had a duty to provide the company with an independent board of directors capable of exercising an impartial judgment, which they failed to do. Furthermore, the company brought the action within a reasonable time and was not barred by laches or acquiescence.","principle":"Promoters are in a fiduciary position towards the company they promote and must provide an independent board of directors and fully disclose material facts when selling property to the company."}},{"id":"c10","t":"Ashbury Railway Carriage and Iron Co. Ltd. v. Riche","cit":"(1875) LR 7 HL 653","court":"House of Lords","topic":"Constitutional documents","sections":["4","13"],"brief":{"facts":"The company was incorporated under the Companies Act 1862. Its memorandum of association listed objects including railway carriages and waggons, general contractors, mines, minerals, land, and timber. The directors entered into a contract to take over a concession to make a railway from Antwerp to Tournay and to furnish funds for the project. Shareholders later appointed a committee that reported the contract was ultra vires.","issue":"Whether a contract made by a company incorporated under the Companies Act 1862, which is beyond the objects stated in its memorandum of association, can be ratified by the shareholders.","held":"The House of Lords held that the contract was ultra vires and void. The court decided that a statutory corporation created under the Act is limited in all its powers by the purposes defined in its memorandum, and the shareholders cannot ratify a contract that is beyond those objects.","principle":"A company incorporated under statute has power only to do those things that are authorized by its memorandum of association, and an ultra vires contract cannot be ratified even by the unanimous consent of all shareholders."}},{"id":"c11","t":"Cotman v. Brougham","cit":"[1918] AC 514","court":"House of Lords","topic":"Constitutional documents","sections":["4","13"],"brief":{"facts":"The Essequibo company was incorporated with a memorandum containing thirty sub-clauses for its objects, along with a concluding clause stating that no sub-clause should be limited by any other and that all powers were independent. The company underwrote and took up shares in another company whose business was completely unrelated to the Essequibo company. Upon the liquidation of that other company, the Essequibo company was placed on the list of contributories and challenged the transaction as ultra vires.","issue":"Whether the underwriting transaction was ultra vires the Essequibo company upon the true construction of its memorandum of association.","held":"The House of Lords held that the transaction was intra vires and dismissed the appeal. Once the registrar has granted a certificate of incorporation, it is conclusive evidence that the requirements of the Act have been complied with, meaning the validity of the memorandum cannot be challenged and must be construed as it stands. Under the wide powers and objects expressed in clauses 3(8), 3(12), and 3(30), the underwriting transaction fell clearly within the company's authorized powers.","principle":"A certificate of incorporation given by the registrar is conclusive evidence that all registration requirements have been complied with, and a transaction challenged as ultra vires must be judged based on the plain construction of the company's memorandum as it stands."}},{"id":"c12","t":"In re (Jon) Beauforte (London) Ltd.","cit":"[1953] Ch 131","court":"Chancery Division","topic":"Constitutional documents","sections":["4","13"],"brief":{"facts":"A company whose memorandum authorized the business of costumiers and gown makers decided to make veneered panels, which was ultra vires. The company erected a factory and incurred debts with builders, veneer suppliers, and coke suppliers. The company went into compulsory liquidation, and the liquidator rejected the creditors' proofs of debt.","issue":"Whether proofs of debt based on ultra vires contracts, or on judgments obtained upon them without litigating or compromising the issue of ultra vires, can be sustained against the company.","held":"The court held that the liquidator rightly rejected the proofs of debt because the underlying contracts were ultra vires and any judgments founded on them were invalid unless they embodied a decision or compromise on the issue of ultra vires. A company cannot make an ultra vires act valid by consenting to a judgment or failing to raise the defense of ultra vires. However, the rejection was made without prejudice to the creditors' rights to trace their money or property or to participate in the distribution of surplus assets.","principle":"No judgment founded on an ultra vires contract can be sustained unless it embodies a decision of the court on the issue of ultra vires, or a compromise of that issue."}},{"id":"c13","t":"Bell Houses Ltd. v. City Wall Properties Ltd.","cit":"[1966] 2 All ER 674","court":"Court of Appeal","topic":"Constitutional documents","sections":["4","13"],"brief":{"facts":"The plaintiff company, Bell Houses, Ltd., was a private company whose principal business was the development of housing estates. During negotiations, the chairman of the plaintiff company, Mr. Bell, agreed to help the defendant company, City Wall Properties, Ltd., secure a financial loan of £1 million in exchange for a commission of £20,000. When the introduction was effected, the defendants refused to pay the commission and argued that the agreement was void as ultra vires because it was not authorised by the objects clause in the plaintiff company's memorandum of association.","issue":"Was the agreement to introduce a financier for a commission ultra vires the plaintiff company under its memorandum of association?","held":"The court decided that the transaction was within the powers of the company and not ultra vires, and therefore allowed the appeal. The court held that the transaction fell within the scope of clause 3(c) of the memorandum because, in the opinion of the directors (exercised here by Mr. Bell), the business could be advantageously carried on in connection with or as ancillary to the general business of the company.","principle":"An act is not ultra vires if it is reasonably incidental or ancillary to the company's objects, particularly where the memorandum empowers the board of directors to decide whether a trade or business can be advantageously carried on in connection with the company's general business."}},{"id":"c14","t":"Re Introductions Ltd.; Introductions Ltd. v. National Provincial Bank Ltd.","cit":"[1969] 1 All ER 887","court":"Court of Appeal","topic":"Constitutional documents","sections":["4","13"],"brief":{"facts":"Introductions Ltd. started a pig-breeding venture, which was outside the powers stated in its memorandum. The company opened an account with the National Provincial Bank that became heavily overdrawn. To secure the overdraft, the bank was given two debentures over the company's assets. The bank knew that the company's sole business was pig-breeding when it took the security.","issue":"Whether the debentures held by the bank were valid or void as ultra vires when the bank knew the borrowed money was for an ultra vires purpose.","held":"The court held that the debentures were void and dismissed the appeal. A power to borrow cannot exist in a vacuum and must be for the purposes of the company, meaning borrowing for an ultra vires purpose is invalid. Furthermore, because the bank had actual knowledge that the money was for an ultra vires purpose, it could not rely on the security.","principle":"A borrowing power in a company memorandum must be for the purposes of the company, and a lender with knowledge that the borrowed money is for an ultra vires purpose cannot enforce security taken for that loan."}},{"id":"c15","t":"Royal British Bank v. Turquand","cit":"(1856) 119 ER 886","court":"Exchequer Chamber","topic":"Constitutional documents","sections":["5","10"],"brief":{"facts":"The plaintiff sued the defendant joint stock company on a bond for £2,000 signed by two directors under the company seal. The registered deed of settlement stated that directors could borrow on bond such sums as a general resolution of the company authorized. The plea stated there was no such resolution authorizing the bond.","issue":"Whether persons dealing with a company are bound to inquire if internal procedural requirements, such as a general resolution to borrow, have been fulfilled.","held":"The Court of Exchequer Chamber held that the plaintiff was entitled to judgment. Parties dealing with joint stock companies are bound to read the statute and the deed of settlement, but they have a right to infer that internal procedural acts like a necessary resolution have been duly carried out.","principle":"Persons dealing with a company are entitled to assume that internal management and procedural requirements of the company have been properly complied with."}},{"id":"c16","t":"Freeman & Lockyer v. Buckhurst Park Properties (Mangal) Ltd.","cit":"[1964] 1 All ER 630","court":"Court of Appeal","topic":"Constitutional documents","sections":["5","10"],"brief":{"facts":"The plaintiffs, who are architects and surveyors, were instructed by the second defendant, Mr. Kapoor, to do work relating to an estate owned by the defendant company. The company was formed to purchase and resell this estate, but Mr. Kapoor was never formally appointed as managing director. The board of directors permitted Mr. Kapoor to act in the management of the company and take steps to find a purchaser without challenge. The plaintiffs completed the work and sued the company for their unpaid fees.","issue":"Whether the defendant company was liable for the fees under a contract made by a director who lacked actual authority, on the basis that he had ostensible or apparent authority to act for the company.","held":"The court held that the defendant company was liable because the second defendant had ostensible authority to employ the plaintiffs. The board of directors, who had actual authority to manage the company, permitted Mr. Kapoor to act as a managing director and conduct the company's business. This conduct represented to the plaintiffs that he had authority to enter into such contracts on behalf of the company, and the company was therefore estopped from denying responsibility.","principle":"A company is bound by a contract entered into by its agent who lacks actual authority if the agent was held out by the board of directors as having ostensible authority to manage that part of the company's business."}},{"id":"c17","t":"Kotla Venkataswamy v. Chinta Ramamurthy","cit":"AIR 1934 Mad 579","court":"Madras High Court","topic":"Constitutional documents","sections":["5","10"],"brief":{"facts":"The plaintiff sued to enforce a mortgage bond executed on behalf of a company by its Working Director and Secretary. The company went into voluntary liquidation and the mortgaged property was purchased by defendant 4. Article 15 of the company required all instruments to be signed by the Managing Director, the Secretary, and the Working Director, but the bond was signed only by the Secretary and the Working Director.","issue":"Whether the mortgage bond was validly executed so as to bind the company when it was signed by only two of the three officers required by the Articles of Association.","held":"The court decided that the mortgage bond was invalid because it lacked the required signature of the Managing Director as mandated by Article 15. The court held that persons dealing with a company have constructive notice of its public documents and articles, so the mortgagee should have known the bond was not properly executed. Since the bond was defective on its face and the required signatures were missing, the plaintiff could not enforce it, and the second appeal was dismissed.","principle":"A person dealing with a company is deemed to have constructive notice of its Companies Act and Articles of Association, and cannot claim protection under the indoor management rule if the defect or illegality is apparent from the documents."}},{"id":"c18","t":"Dehradun-Mussoorie Electric Tramway Co. v. Jagmandar Das","cit":"AIR 1932 All 141","court":"Allahabad High Court","topic":"Constitutional documents","sections":["5","10"],"brief":{"facts":"The plaintiffs gave an overdraft of Rs. 25,000 to the Dehradun Mussoorie Electric Tramway Company through its managing agent, Mr. Beltie Shah Gilani, which was used for necessary company purposes. A mortgage over company land was later executed by the managing agent to secure this overdraft. The company subsequently went into liquidation, and the defendants disputed the validity of the mortgage while admitting the debt.","issue":"Whether the mortgage deed executed by the managing agent to secure the company's overdraft is valid and binding upon the company.","held":"The court held that the mortgage was void because it transferred part of the company's undertaking without the previous assent of the Government, as required by the Tramway Order. However, because the loan was genuine and used for company purposes, the court granted the plaintiffs a simple money decree to rank as unsecured creditors.","principle":"A mortgage of a tramway undertaking made in contravention of statutory provisions requiring previous government sanction is absolutely void and cannot be ratified."}},{"id":"c19","t":"Percival v. Wright","cit":"[1902] 2 Ch 421","court":"Chancery Division","topic":"Board of Directors","sections":["166"],"brief":{"facts":"The plaintiffs were joint registered owners of shares in a colliery company. The plaintiffs initiated the sale of their shares to the chairman and other directors after naming their desired price. During the negotiations, the directors were also being approached by a third party with a view to purchasing the entire undertaking of the company, but the directors did not disclose these negotiations to the plaintiffs.","issue":"Whether the directors of a company were under a fiduciary duty to disclose pending negotiations for the sale of the company's undertaking when purchasing shares from an individual shareholder.","held":"The court decided that the sale of shares was valid and dismissed the action. The court held that directors are not trustees for individual shareholders and are under no obligation to disclose to a vendor shareholder ongoing negotiations for the sale of the company's undertaking.","principle":"Directors of a company are not trustees for individual shareholders and may purchase their shares without disclosing pending negotiations for the sale of the company's undertaking."}},{"id":"c20","t":"Burland v. Earle","cit":"[1902] AC 83","court":"Privy Council","topic":"Board of Directors","sections":["166","245"],"brief":{"facts":"The British American Bank Note Company was a highly successful joint stock company incorporated in Canada. Over many years, the company accumulated a large amount of undivided profits on its profit and loss account instead of distributing them all as dividends. Minority shareholders brought an action against the company, the president George B. Burland, and other directors, challenging the retention of reserve funds, certain investments, the resale of property to the company at a profit, and specific salary payments.","issue":"Whether the court can interfere with the internal management of a company regarding the retention and investment of undivided profits, and whether a director must account for profits made on property previously purchased privately and resold to the company.","held":"The court decided that the accumulation and investment of undivided profits are matters of internal management for the shareholders to decide, and the court has no jurisdiction to interfere. Furthermore, a director who purchases property independently and later resells it to the company cannot be forced in a minority shareholder action to account merely for the profit on resale without setting aside the contract. However, the court held that the president must repay certain salary amounts improperly drawn under an ambiguous resolution that did not apply to him.","principle":"Courts will not interfere with the internal management of companies acting within their powers, and shareholders must decide whether profits should be divided or retained as a reserve fund."}},{"id":"c22","t":"Regal (Hastings) Ltd. v. Gulliver","cit":"[1967] 2 AC 134","court":"House of Lords","topic":"Board of Directors","sections":["166"],"brief":{"facts":"Regal formed a subsidiary company called Amalgamated to acquire leases for two cinemas. When the owners demanded a rent guarantee, the directors decided Regal could only invest £2,000 and that the remaining shares should be taken up by the directors and the solicitor. These shares were later sold at a substantial profit, and Regal sued to recover those profits.","issue":"Are directors and a solicitor accountable to their company for profits made on the acquisition and sale of shares in a subsidiary company, when they used their positions to acquire the shares and acted in good faith without fraud?","held":"The directors, other than Gulliver, are liable to account for the profits because they used their fiduciary position and exclusive knowledge as directors to acquire the shares. The absence of fraud or bad faith does not excuse them from liability. The claims against Gulliver and Garton failed because Gulliver made no profit and Garton took his shares with the full knowledge and consent of Regal.","principle":"A person in a fiduciary position, such as a director, must not make a profit by using their fiduciary position or knowledge acquired in it, and if they do, they must account for that profit even if the company could not have acquired the property itself and the directors acted in good faith."}},{"id":"c23","t":"Industrial Development Consultants Ltd. v. Cooley","cit":"[1972] 1 WLR 443","court":"Chancery Division","topic":"Board of Directors","sections":["166"],"brief":{"facts":"The defendant Neville Cooley was the managing director of the plaintiff company, Industrial Development Consultants Ltd. While still acting as managing director, he was approached by a representative of the Eastern Gas Board regarding a valuable construction project that the plaintiff company had previously tried to secure. The defendant obtained a release from his position by falsely and dishonestly pretending to Mr. Hicks that he was in poor health, and he subsequently secured the lucrative gas board contract for himself.","issue":"Whether a managing director, who acquires business information and a contract opportunity while acting for his company, is accountable to the company for the profits made even if the company could not have obtained the contract itself.","held":"The court decided that the defendant must account to the plaintiff company for all profits and remuneration received from the contracts. The court held that the defendant breached his fiduciary duty by placing himself in a position where his personal interest and his duty to his employer conflicted, and by misusing information obtained while acting as managing director.","principle":"A person in a fiduciary position, such as a company director, is subject to an inflexible rule of equity not to make a profit from their trust and must not place themselves in a position where their duty and personal interest conflict."}},{"id":"c24","t":"Foss v. Harbottle","cit":"(1843) 67 ER 189; 2 Hare 461","court":"Chancery Court","topic":"Prevention of oppression and mismanagement","sections":["241","242","245"],"brief":{"facts":"Two shareholders of the Victoria Park Company filed a bill on behalf of themselves and other shareholders against the directors, solicitor, architect, and others, charging them with fraudulent and illegal transactions that misapplied and wasted company property. The bill alleged that the company had no clerk or office, that there was an insufficient number of qualified directors, and that the proprietors had no power to take the property out of the hands of the defendants or wind up the affairs. The defendants demurred to the bill.","issue":"Whether individual shareholders can sue in their own names to redress wrongs done to the company when the company itself has the power to obtain redress through a general meeting.","held":"The court held that the demurrers must be allowed because the allegations in the bill did not exclude the possibility of convening a general meeting of proprietors capable of controlling the existing board. Since the company could obtain redress in its corporate character, individual plaintiffs could not sue in a form that assumed the practical dissolution of the corporation.","principle":"A court will not interfere with the internal management of a company at the suit of a minority of shareholders if the majority can ratify the act or if the company itself can seek redress."}},{"id":"c25","t":"Shanti Prasad Jain v. Kalinga Tubes Ltd.","cit":"AIR 1965 SC 1535","court":"Supreme Court of India","topic":"Prevention of oppression and mismanagement","sections":["241","242"],"brief":{"facts":"The company was floated as a private limited company in 1950, with shares held equally by two groups. In 1954, the appellant agreed to provide finance, loans, and administrative guidance, leading to an agreement where the company would have three groups of shareholders holding equal shares. Later, the company was converted into a public company, and differences arose regarding the issue of new shares, resulting in a breach between the groups and subsequent allotment of new shares to exclude the appellant's group.","issue":"Whether the affairs of the company were being conducted in a manner oppressive to the appellant and his group, or prejudicial to the interests of the company, and whether there was mismanagement justifying court intervention.","held":"The court held that the company was not a party to the 1954 agreement and was not bound by it, so failure to carry out the agreement did not constitute oppression. Furthermore, the delay in receiving full share money, the withdrawal of a sum owed to the former managing agent, and the loss of the appellant's support did not amount to prejudicial conduct or mismanagement of the company. Therefore, the appeals were dismissed.","principle":"A private agreement between certain shareholders to which the company is not a party cannot bind the company, and mere exclusion from control or a breakdown of trust does not amount to oppression or mismanagement under the Act unless specific statutory grounds are proven."}},{"id":"c26","t":"Rajahmundry Electric Supply Corporation Ltd. v. A. Nageshwara Rao","cit":"AIR 1956 SC 213","court":"Supreme Court of India","topic":"Prevention of oppression and mismanagement","sections":["241","242"],"brief":{"facts":"An application was filed under Section 162 of the Companies Act to wind up the Rajahmundry Electric Supply Corporation Ltd. due to alleged gross mismanagement, misappropriation of funds by directors, and large unpaid electricity charges owed to the Government. The applicant also alternatively sought relief under Section 153-C. The appellant argued that the Section 153-C application was not maintainable because some consenting shareholders had subsequently withdrawn their consent.","issue":"Whether the application under Section 153-C was maintainable after some members withdrew their consent, and whether the established facts justified a winding up order under Section 162(vi) so that action could be taken under Section 153-C.","held":"The Supreme Court held that the validity of a petition must be judged on the facts at the time of its presentation, and subsequent withdrawal of consent by members does not affect its maintainability. The Court also held that the words 'just and equitable' in Section 162(vi) are not ejusdem generis with preceding clauses, and since the company suffered from severe mismanagement, a local junta ruling, and financial embarrassment, a winding up order and subsequent management under Section 153-C were fully justified.","principle":"The validity of a winding up petition is determined by the facts existing at the time of its presentation and cannot be defeated by the subsequent withdrawal of consent by members. Furthermore, the 'just和 equitable' ground for winding up is not restricted by the ejusdem generis rule and allows the court to intervene in cases of severe director misconduct and mismanagement."}},{"id":"c27","t":"Bharat Insurance Co. Ltd. v. Kanhaya Lal Gauba","cit":"AIR 1935 Lah 792","court":"Lahore High Court","topic":"Prevention of oppression and mismanagement","sections":["241","242"],"brief":{"facts":"The plaintiff, a shareholder and director of the Bharat Insurance Company, filed a suit alleging that the company's directors were investing life insurance funds in business undertakings controlled by the Chairman without adequate security. The plaintiff sought a declaration regarding the true construction of Clause 3(d) of the Memorandum of Association and a perpetual injunction restraining the company and directors from granting loans or making investments without proper security and quorum. The lower court granted the declaration and a perpetual injunction against the directors, leading to the company's appeal.","issue":"Whether a single member of a company can maintain a suit for a declaration regarding the interpretation of the Memorandum of Association, and how Clause 3(d) regarding advancing money and investing money should be correctly interpreted.","held":"The court held that a single member can maintain an action to prevent ultra vires acts and obtain a declaration on the true construction of the Memorandum of Association when directors misinterpret company powers. The court set aside the perpetual injunction because the directors were not personally impleaded and there was no reason to assume future regular conduct would be breached. The court modified the declaration to distinguish between advancing money as loans—which requires property security in India—and investing money not immediately required, where directors have discretion over securities.","principle":"A single member of a company can maintain a suit against the company for a declaration concerning the true construction of the Memorandum of Association to prevent ultra vires acts, as this is not a matter of mere internal management."}},{"id":"c28","t":"M.S.D.C. Radharamanan v. M.S.D. Chandrasekara Raja","cit":"(2008) 6 SCC 750; AIR 2008 SC 1738","court":"Supreme Court of India","topic":"Prevention of oppression and mismanagement","sections":["241","242","244"],"brief":{"facts":"M/s Shree Bhaarathi Cotton Mills Private Limited is a company where nearly all equity shares are held by the first respondent and his son, the appellant. The first respondent serves as the Managing Director and the appellant as a Director, but the parties are not on good terms and bitterness has crept into their personal relationship. The first respondent filed an application under Sections 397 and 398 of the Companies Act, 1956 before the Company Law Board alleging oppression, and the Company Law Board found no mala fide or oppression but concluded there was a deadlock and ordered the appellant to buy the first respondent's shares. On appeal, the High Court dismissed the appeal and upheld the direction with modifications regarding the valuation and purchase mechanism.","issue":"Whether the Company Law Board and the High Court were justified in exercising their jurisdiction under Section 402 of the Companies Act to order the purchase of shares upon finding a management deadlock, despite concluding that no actual act of oppression had been committed by the appellant.","held":"The Supreme Court held that the Company Law Board and the High Court rightly exercised their jurisdiction to direct the purchase of shares. The Court reasoned that where a company is in the nature of a quasi-partnership with only two directors whose non-cooperation and acrimony result in a stalemate and mismanagement, the Company Law Board is not powerless and can grant appropriate reliefs under Section 402 to safeguard the company's interest and ensure its smooth functioning, even if strict oppression is not proved. Consequently, the appeal was dismissed.","principle":"The Company Law Board has wide discretionary powers under Section 402 read with Sections 397 and 398 of the Companies Act to pass beneficial orders—such as directing the purchase of shares—to resolve management deadlocks and protect the interests of the company, even if a strict case of oppression is not established."}},{"id":"c29","t":"Tata Consultancy Services Ltd. v. Cyrus Investments Pvt. Ltd.","cit":"(2021) 9 SCC 449","court":"Supreme Court of India","topic":"Prevention of oppression and mismanagement","sections":["241","242","244"],"brief":{"facts":"Cyrus Pallonji Mistry was removed from the post of Executive Chairman of Tata Sons Private Limited by a board resolution in October 2016. Following his removal, companies belonging to the Shapoorji Pallonji Group filed a petition under Sections 241 and 242 of the Companies Act, 2013, alleging oppression and mismanagement. The National Company Law Appellate Tribunal ruled in favour of the complainants, granting various reliefs including Mistry's reinstatement, which led to multiple appeals before the Supreme Court of India.","issue":"Whether the removal of Cyrus Pallonji Mistry as Executive Chairman and the corporate governance practices of Tata Sons amounted to oppression and mismanagement under Sections 241 and 242 of the Companies Act, 2013.","held":"The Supreme Court allowed the appeals filed by the Tata Group and dismissed the appeal and applications of the Shapoorji Pallonji Group, thereby setting aside the NCLAT order. The Court held that the removal of the Executive Chairman was in accordance with the articles of association and corporate governance rules, and that no case of oppression of minority shareholders or mismanagement of company affairs was made out.","principle":"A company's board of directors has the inherent power to remove an executive chairman based on loss of confidence, provided it follows the articles of association, and minority shareholders cannot claim oppression merely because a valid corporate decision runs contrary to their wishes."}},{"id":"c30","t":"In re German Date Coffee Co.","cit":"(1882) 20 Ch D 169","court":"Court of Appeal","topic":"Winding up of companies","sections":["271"],"brief":{"facts":"A company was formed to work a German patent for manufacturing coffee from dates, with a memorandum of association containing general clauses for acquiring other patents and carrying on related businesses. The intended German patent was never granted, although the company purchased a Swedish patent and began making and selling date coffee in Hamburg without a patent. Two shareholders petitioned to wind up the company on the ground that its objects had entirely failed.","issue":"Whether it is just and equitable to wind up a company under the Companies Act when the main patent and primary object for which it was formed have failed.","held":"The court held that the substratum of the company had failed and it was impossible to carry out the objects for which it was formed, making it just and equitable to wind up the company. The court reasoned that the true object of the company was to work a specific German patent, not to carry on a general business without a patent.","principle":"If the whole substratum of a company—the specific business and main purpose for which it was incorporated—has become impossible to achieve, the court may order the company to be wound up under the just and equitable clause."}},{"id":"c31","t":"Seth Mohan Lal v. Grain Chambers Ltd.","cit":"AIR 1968 SC 772","court":"Supreme Court of India","topic":"Winding up of companies","sections":["271"],"brief":{"facts":"The Grain Chamber Ltd. was formed to carry on business in various commodities, principally trading in futures in gur in 1949 and 1950. After government orders banned future contracts in gur in February 1950, the company passed resolutions to settle outstanding contracts at the closing market rate of February 14, 1950. The appellants filed a winding-up petition, claiming the company was unable to pay debts, that directors committed fraud, and that the substratum of the company was destroyed.","issue":"Whether the company should be wound up on the grounds that it is unable to pay its debts, that directors committed fraudulent acts, or that its substratum has disappeared making it just and equitable to wind it up.","held":"The Supreme Court held that the company was not unable to pay its debts, the directors acted prudently without fraud, and the substratum of the company had not disappeared because it was formed to trade in multiple commodities, not gur alone. The court ruled that the company possessed sufficient assets to restart business and that no case was made out to order a winding up.","principle":"A company will not be ordered to be wound up on just and equitable grounds unless its main object has substantially failed, it cannot carry on business except at a loss, or its assets are insufficient to meet its liabilities."}},{"id":"c32","t":"Madras Bar Association v. Union of India","cit":"Writ Petition (C) No. 1072 of 2013, decided 14 May 2015","court":"Supreme Court of India","topic":"Adjudicatory bodies","sections":["408","409","410","411","412"],"brief":{"facts":"The Madras Bar Association filed a writ petition challenging the constitutional validity of several provisions of Chapter XXVII of the Companies Act, 2013 regarding the National Company Law Tribunal and the National Company Law Appellate Tribunal. The petitioner argued that despite earlier directions given by the Supreme Court in the 2010 Madras Bar Association judgment, the new statutory provisions in the 2013 Act remained almost identical and suffered from unconstitutionality. The court examined the challenges regarding the composition of the Selection Committee, qualifications, and other incidental powers of the tribunals.","issue":"Whether the provisions of Chapter XXVII of the Companies Act, 2013 relating to the constitution, qualifications, selection committee structure, and terms of the National Company Law Tribunal and National Company Law Appellate Tribunal violate Article 14 of the Constitution in light of the binding 2010 judgment.","held":"The Supreme Court partly allowed the writ petition. It held that the composition of the Selection Committee under Section 412(2) of the Companies Act, 2013 is invalid and not in accord with the 2010 Constitution Bench judgment because it gives a predominant say to administrative bureaucrats over judicial members and lacks a casting vote for the Chairperson. The court directed that the defect in the Selection Committee composition be removed by bringing it in line with the earlier binding precedent.","principle":"Statutory provisions governing the structure, qualifications, and selection committees of specialized tribunals must strictly conform to previously laid down constitutional benchmarks and binding judicial precedents to ensure judicial independence."}},{"id":"c33","t":"Embassy Property Developments Pvt. Ltd. v. State of Karnataka","cit":"MANU/SC/1661/2019","court":"Supreme Court of India","topic":"Adjudicatory bodies","sections":["408"],"brief":{"facts":"A financial creditor initiated the corporate insolvency resolution process against the corporate debtor before the NCLT. The corporate debtor held a mining lease granted by the Government of Karnataka, which the State government later refused to extend due to rule violations. The NCLT allowed an application by the resolution professional and set aside the State's rejection order, directing execution of supplemental lease deeds. The Government of Karnataka challenged this before the High Court via a writ petition, which granted an interim stay.","issue":"Whether the NCLT has the jurisdiction to review administrative decisions made under public law enactments like the MMDR Act, 1957, and whether the High Court was justified in entertaining a writ petition against the NCLT's order.","held":"The Supreme Court held that the NCLT is a creature of a special statute and cannot be elevated to the status of a superior court with the power of judicial review over administrative actions in the realm of public law. Decisions by statutory authorities under the MMDR Act, 1957, fall outside the NCLT's jurisdiction, and the resolution professional cannot use the NCLT as a bypass to enforce rights outside the IBC. Therefore, the NCLT lacked jurisdiction, and the High Court was fully justified in entertaining the writ petition.","principle":"The NCLT does not possess the power of judicial review over administrative actions and public law disputes, such as those arising under the MMDR Act, 1957, as it is not a superior court."}}],"extra":[{"t":"State Trading Corporation v. CTO","cit":"AIR 1963 SC 811","topic":"Nature and kinds of companies"},{"t":"TELCO v. State of Bihar","cit":"AIR 1965 SC 40","topic":"Nature and kinds of companies"},{"t":"R.C. Cooper v. Union of India","cit":"(1970) 3 SCR 530","topic":"Nature and kinds of companies"},{"t":"CIT v. Meenakshi Mills Ltd.","cit":"AIR 1967 SC 819","topic":"Nature and kinds of companies"},{"t":"Kapila Hingorani v. State of Bihar","cit":"(2003) 6 SCC 1","topic":"Nature and kinds of companies"},{"t":"Dr. A. Lakshmanaswami Mudaliar v. Life Insurance Corporation of India","cit":"AIR 1963 SC 1185","topic":"Constitutional documents"},{"t":"Shuttleworth v. Cox Brothers and Company (Maidenhead) Ltd.","cit":"[1927] 2 K.B. 9","topic":"Constitutional documents"},{"t":"Rayfield v. Hands","cit":"[1957 R. No. 603]","topic":"Constitutional documents"},{"t":"Jehangir Rastamji Modi v. Shamji Ladha","cit":"(1867) BHCR 185","topic":"Constitutional documents"},{"t":"City Equitable Fire Insurance Co., Re","cit":"(1925) Ch. 407","topic":"Board of Directors"},{"t":"Standard Chartered Bank v. Pakistan National Shipping Corp.","cit":"(2003) 1 All ER 173 (HL)","topic":"Board of Directors"},{"t":"Sanjay Dutt v. State of Haryana","cit":"Supreme Court of India, 2 January 2025, Criminal Appeal No. 11 of 2025","topic":"Board of Directors"},{"t":"H.R. Harmer Ltd., Re","cit":"(1958) 3 All E.R. 689","topic":"Prevention of oppression and mismanagement"},{"t":"Scottish Co-operative Wholesale Society Ltd. v. Meyer","cit":"1959 AC 324","topic":"Prevention of oppression and mismanagement"},{"t":"Needle Industries (India) Ltd. v. Needle Industries Newey (India) Holdings Ltd.","cit":"AIR 1981 SC 1298","topic":"Prevention of oppression and mismanagement"},{"t":"Aluminum Corporation of India Ltd. v. Lakshmi Rattan Cotton Mills Co. Ltd.","cit":"AIR 1970 All 452","topic":"Winding up of companies"},{"t":"Yenidje Tobacco Co. Ltd., Re","cit":"(1916) 2 Ch. D. 169","topic":"Winding up of companies"},{"t":"Devas Employees Mauritius Private Limited v. Union of India","cit":"Karnataka High Court, W.P. No. 6191 of 2021, 28 April 2021","topic":"Winding up of companies"},{"t":"Arun Kumar Jagatramka v. Jindal Steel and Power Ltd.","cit":"AIR 2021 SC 136","topic":"Contemporary developments"}]};
